periment of getting a random outcome  and that a third of the time you get a  that is an observable part of the  quantum theory and so how is many worlds  going to reproduce that kind of  randomness that's the question for  today's video the most popular way to  resolve this issue is to kind of forego  uncertainty altogether and instead  define the probabilities in terms of  like uh betting and things like that  while i think that that's all very  elegant i think that i would prefer a  understanding of probability that is a  little bit closer to my intuitive  understanding of probability and so  that's why in this video i'm going to  talk about a more straightforward way to  bring uncertainty into the many worlds  theory and how that leads to the  probabilities that you would expect from  quantum mechanics but before i talk  about what i think is the right answer  i'd like to first talk about what i  think is not the right answer even  though it is very appealing and that is  where if you want to find the  probability of a you just count the  number of worlds where a happens over  the total number of worlds that there  are and that is your probability of a  that seems like it would be a great  solution but unfortunately it's just not  we're going to see in the following  example that if you try and assign  probabilities that way that you run into  inconsistencies where you try and find  out the probability by doing the  measurement one way and you get a  certain answer and then you try and find  out the probability by doing the  measurement a different way and you get  a different answer that theory just  can't work um and then we'll go on to  how to fix it  imagine i have something like an  electron and that electron could be  in one of  three possible energy levels you could  imagine this as like energy levels  inside of an atom or something like that  now classically if we had a situation  like this then the electron would be  doing one of those three but in quantum  mechanics there is the possibility of a  superposition what that means is the  electron will be in some sense doing all  three of those things at the same time  but then let's say that i want to go and  measure the electrons energy  what is going to be now the probability  of getting for example a it seems like  all three of them should just be a third  because there are three worlds and why  not assume that they're all equally  likely and so the probabilities should  all be a third but we're gonna see that  that can't be right all the time let's  say that instead of just doing this a b  c measurement you decide to do a  slightly more complicated measurement  and this is the measurement of whether  the state is a or whether it's x  x means an energy higher than a so in  this case that would be b or c this is a  perfectly valid measurement to do so  you're just trying to figure out is the  energy a or is it higher than a and that  is allowed in quantum mechanics so since  there are two outcomes our rule tells us  that the probabilities of both of them  ought to be a half but we don't have to  stop there if we get the outcome x then  we can do a further measurement to  figure out if the energy was b or  whether it was c there are two outcomes  here as well and so those probabilities  should be a half and a half again  according to our rule that all outcomes  should be equally likely but now you've  reached a contradiction because this  whole thing is just a really convoluted  way of doing that same measurement that  you were going to do the first time  you're just figuring out whether the  energy is a b or c  and so these two ways of measuring the  energy ought to give you the same  results and in regular quantum mechanics  they do but here you can see they don't  according to this tree the probability  of finding the outcome c  is a half times a half  same with b  and probability of a is actually a half  so you can see there's this  inconsistency when you try and figure  out the probabilities by doing branch  counting so that means that the  probability in the many worlds  interpretation can't be to do with just  the number of worlds even though that  seems like it should be it to really  drive that home let's look at an example  where there are just two outcomes and  that means that there are just two  worlds and yet we'll find that in some  sense the probability of one of those  worlds is a lot greater than the  probability of another world let's say  that we have a quantum state like this  so don't worry if you haven't seen this  kind of notation before all it's saying  is that we have some sort of particle  and that particle is red  and blue at the same time in what's  called the quantum superposition but in  some sense it's more blue than it is red  because that number in front of the blue  bit is higher than the number in front  of the red bit and the way that would  manifest itself in regular quantum  mechanics is via what's called the born  rule the born rule just says that if you  were to do a measurement of whether the  particle is red or blue in regular  quantum mechanics it would collapse to  just doing one of those so you would  only see one of those colors and the  probability that it is red would be  equal to a third which is  something you get by taking that number  and taking the modulus squared of it  because that number was smaller that  probability is smaller  but how is this going to work in the  many worlds theory where if you were to  do a measurement of this red and blue  particle then you would see both those  outcomes there would be like two  versions of yourself one who sees red  one who sees blue let's say this is you  and you get one of these particles  that's just a representation of the  particle before you measure it because  it's both blue and red but in some sense  it's more blue than red if you were to  actually do this experiment you wouldn't  see both red and blue instead you would  get the situation where you essentially  split into two one version of you would  see the red outcome and the other would  see the blue so i get rid of this  because that state is now gone by the  way it's a very important fact that you  will only be able to experience one of  the worlds even if you know on a  theoretical level that the other world  exists you won't be able to see any  evidence of them you won't be able to  see both a blue particle and a red  particle you'll only be able to see one  outcome it's a really important feature  of many worlds that this is true that  you can only experience one world and  that's basically what is making this  proof work and so if you want to see the  argument for why that's true i've made a  whole video about it but if you're happy  to accept that then let's keep going  with this proof if we were to think  about this from a branch counting point  of view you can see that there are in  fact only two worlds and so you know the  probability of red ought to be half and  the probability of blue ought to be a  half so how are we going to recover the  one-third and two-thirds what does  probability even mean in the  circumstance where both things happen on  metal level there is no uncertainty in  this experiment because you know that  when you do it you are going to split  into two different people and one is  going to see red and one is going to see  blue but on the level of your experience  there actually is a lot of uncertainty  you see each person can only  see one of those outcomes these two  people even though they are both future  versions of that person are now separate  people they're living separate lives  with separate experiences as evidenced  by this they're seeing different things  and are from now on basically two  different people who cannot interact and  can't see the other world but this  person who is in the present there is  some uncertainty and that uncertainty is  what world am i going to experience in  other 