 times you saw red versus how many times  you saw blue you should find that about  a third of the time you get red and  two-thirds of the time you get blue  so that's probability okay wait a second  i've defined probability and what i mean  by probability in a way that i feel like  makes sense it's sort of about like how  often you can expect to have the  experience of getting into a particular  world or another one but i haven't  proved why that number should equal a  third or two-thirds in this case that is  actually something that you can prove  and i think it's really nice like  actually very simple proof but i've  decided to put the proof of that born  rule into the next video because  interestingly enough that proof doesn't  just apply to many worlds it also works  equally well in the sort of standard  copenhagen interpretation of quantum  mechanics uh weirdly the proof just  doesn't really care about any of the  details of how you define probability as  long as you have defined something  called probability which is essentially  a function that takes your state and  gives you back a number as long as there  is such a thing and you're searching for  it then there is only one function that  will work and that is the modulus  squared function and that's just because  of consistency we talked about how like  if you were assigning probabilities  according to the branch counting rule  then you run into the problem of  a lack of consistency between different  ways of doing measurements like you can  do the measurement one way and get one  outcome and do the measurement another  way get a different outcome so if we  just enforce that the probability rule  that we want is consistent then there is  only one function that fits that bill  and that is the regular born rule so um  i think that's a really neat proof and  so i've decided to just like separate  that out because i think that people  will be interested in that  whether or not they care about many  words so yeah if you want to see that  it's over there

